language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ zebra: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The Zebra puzzle (sometimes referred to as Einstein's puzzle) is defined as follows. There are five houses in a row, numbered from left to right. Each of the five houses is painted a different color, and has one inhabitant. The inhabitants are all of different nationalities, own different pets, drink different beverages and have different jobs. Colors are yellow, green, red, white, and blue; nations of inhabitants are italy, spain, japan, england, and norway; pets are cat, zebra, bear, snails, and horse; drinks are milk, water, tea, coffee, and juice; jobs are painter, sculptor, diplomat, pianist, and doctor. The clues are: the painter owns the horse; the diplomat drinks coffee; the one who drinks milk lives in the white house; the Spaniard is a painter; the Englishman lives in the red house; the snails are owned by the sculptor; the green house is immediately on the left of the red one; the Norwegian lives immediately on the right of the blue house; the doctor drinks milk; the diplomat is Japanese; the Norwegian owns the zebra; the green house is next to the white one; the horse is owned by the neighbor of the diplomat; the Italian either lives in the red, white or green house. The numbers 0 to 4 give the house of each inhabitant, and the same number represents the same house.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/zebra.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ the house numbers run from 0 (the leftmost house) to 4 (the rightmost house)
letting last_house = 4
$ the positions of the five attributes of each kind, numbered as the statement lists them
letting yellow = 1
letting green = 2
letting red = 3
letting white = 4
letting blue = 5
letting italy = 1
letting spain = 2
letting japan = 3
letting england = 4
letting norway = 5
letting cat = 1
letting zebra = 2
letting bear = 3
letting snails = 4
letting horse = 5
letting milk = 1
letting water = 2
letting tea = 3
letting coffee = 4
letting juice = 5
letting painter = 1
letting sculptor = 2
letting diplomat = 3
letting pianist = 4
letting doctor = 5

$ colors[c]: the house of the colour c (1 yellow, 2 green, 3 red, 4 white, 5 blue)
find colors : matrix indexed by [int(1..5)] of int(0..last_house)
$ nations[n]: the house of the nationality n (1 Italian, 2 Spaniard, 3 Japanese, 4 Englishman, 5 Norwegian)
find nations : matrix indexed by [int(1..5)] of int(0..last_house)
$ pets[p]: the house of the pet p (1 cat, 2 zebra, 3 bear, 4 snails, 5 horse)
find pets : matrix indexed by [int(1..5)] of int(0..last_house)
$ drinks[d]: the house of the drink d (1 milk, 2 water, 3 tea, 4 coffee, 5 juice)
find drinks : matrix indexed by [int(1..5)] of int(0..last_house)
$ jobs[j]: the house of the job j (1 painter, 2 sculptor, 3 diplomat, 4 pianist, 5 doctor)
find jobs : matrix indexed by [int(1..5)] of int(0..last_house)

such that
$ the five colours are in different houses
allDiff(colors),
$ the five nationalities are in different houses
allDiff(nations),
$ the five pets are in different houses
allDiff(pets),
$ the five drinks are in different houses
allDiff(drinks),
$ the five jobs are in different houses
allDiff(jobs),
$ the painter owns the horse
jobs[painter] = pets[horse],
$ the diplomat drinks coffee
jobs[diplomat] = drinks[coffee],
$ the one who drinks milk lives in the white house
drinks[milk] = colors[white],
$ the Spaniard is a painter
nations[spain] = jobs[painter],
$ the Englishman lives in the red house
nations[england] = colors[red],
$ the snails are owned by the sculptor
pets[snails] = jobs[sculptor],
$ the green house is immediately on the left of the red one
colors[green] + 1 = colors[red],
$ the Norwegian lives immediately on the right of the blue house
nations[norway] = colors[blue] + 1,
$ the doctor drinks milk
jobs[doctor] = drinks[milk],
$ the diplomat is Japanese
jobs[diplomat] = nations[japan],
$ the Norwegian owns the zebra
nations[norway] = pets[zebra],
$ the green house is next to the white one
(colors[green] + 1 = colors[white] \/ colors[white] + 1 = colors[green]),
$ the horse is owned by the neighbour of the diplomat
(pets[horse] + 1 = jobs[diplomat] \/ jobs[diplomat] + 1 = pets[horse]),
$ the Italian either lives in the red, white or green house
(nations[italy] = colors[red] \/ nations[italy] = colors[white] \/ nations[italy] = colors[green])
